event('212-5.4','used and rechecked in this run','With the intended admissibility criterion of 5.3, saturated affine neighbourhoods cover X and their affine quotients cover Y. Applying the effective finite locally free relation assertion 5.1(iv) on each gives R1 = X times_Y X and f finite locally free; these properties and the canonical isomorphism glue. The substantial algebraic assertion 5.1(iv) is still the background input identified on p. 111, not an independent proof supplied by this corollary.','212, p. 112, Corollary 5.4')
event('212-5.5','independent re-proof','Conditional on 5.4, f is finite locally free and surjective, hence faithfully flat. Its pullback along itself is p1:R1 to X, so the rank at x equals the rank of f at f(x); surjectivity proves the rank equivalence. The common Z-map on X descends uniquely through the coequalizer, giving Y its Z-structure. If Y is flat over Z, composition with flat f makes X flat. Conversely, locally on Z and on an affine open of Y, write its inverse image as Spec B over Spec A with B faithfully flat over A. If B is flat over the base ring D, then for every injection of D-modules the induced map after tensor with A becomes injective after tensor with B; faithful flatness detects its kernel, so A is flat over D. This proves descent of flatness here.','212, p. 112, Corollary 5.5')
event('212-5-scholie','targeted proof check','A finite locally free surjection has effective kernel relation by faithful flat descent; its projections are its base changes. Each fibre lies in the affine inverse image of an affine neighbourhood in Y. Conversely an equivalence relation satisfying the printed finite locally free and affine-orbit conditions has the quotient of 5.3 and effectiveness of 5.4. Uniqueness is the coequalizer property. The reverse implication depends on the already identified background effectivity theorem; the forward faithful-flat equalizer is the standard descent input.','212, p. 112, Scholie')
event('212-5.6-1','carried as background without fault found','The finite locally free quotient statements are presented without a noetherian hypothesis. The inspected norm, localization and affine-neighbourhood arguments did not use noetherianity; the remaining effectivity theorem must likewise be sourced in its nonnoetherian form in the notes.','212, p. 112, Remark 5.6(1)')
event('212-5.6-2','targeted proof check','In the explicit exponent-one p-basis model B=A[t1,...,tr]/(ti^p-ai), with pA=0 and the p-monomials an A-basis, the coordinate derivations are well-defined and their common kernel is exactly A: differentiating the basis expansion makes every coefficient of a nonconstant monomial vanish, since each exponent 1,...,p-1 is a unit. Thus this finite free special case realizes an inseparable quotient without regularity or finite-type-over-a-field assumptions. This checks the elementary model only; the complete Cartier correspondence and the historical Jacobson-Bourbaki identification are carried as background and need exact later locators. The statement is not silently restricted to ti^p=0.','212, p. 112, Remark 5.6(2)')
event('212-5.6-3','carried as background without fault found','The attribution to Gabriel and forward reference (7.4) are retained exactly. No independent historical priority verification is claimed; the mathematical specialization and the target reference will be checked when that section is reached.','212, p. 112, Remark 5.6(3)')
